<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Purandara Dasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1484–1564</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Purandara Dasa was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> poet-saint and composer of the Haridasa devotional movement who lived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>. A wealthy merchant before he gave away his property, he composed thousands of devotional songs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. He systematised the teaching of Carnatic music, devising graded exercises still used by beginners, and is traditionally called the father of Carnatic music.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/purandara-dasa.jpg" alt="Purandara Dasa" width="230" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Music and dance on stamps · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:481_Purandardasa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
